# Mount attached filesystems for the Tributary console.
#
# Raspberry Pi OS Lite has no automounter: udisks2 ships in the base image
# and runs, but it only mounts when a client calls Filesystem.Mount, and a
# headless install has no such client. Without this rule a plugged-in drive
# never reaches the mount table, and the console's destination list — which
# reads the mount table — stays blind to it.
#
# This rule used to require ID_BUS=usb. That excluded the SD card the
# appliance boots from, which was the point, but it also excluded every
# NVMe SSD — the best medium this recorder can write to. The bus a drive
# hangs off is a description, not a permission, so the filter is gone and
# drive-mount.sh asks the real question instead: is this the disk the
# appliance runs from? That guard holds however the Pi booted, which the
# bus test never did.
#
# ID_FS_USAGE=filesystem admits both partitions and whole disks written
# without a partition table.

# `change`, not just `add`: it is what a card reader emits when the card is
# swapped without replugging the reader, and what the kernel emits once
# mkfs closes the device — which is how a drive formatted from the console
# remounts itself with no physical replug. It also means this rule fires
# more than once per device, so the helper's already-mounted guard is
# load-bearing rather than defensive.
ACTION!="add|change",           GOTO="tributary_storage_end"
SUBSYSTEM!="block",             GOTO="tributary_storage_end"
ENV{ID_FS_USAGE}!="filesystem", GOTO="tributary_storage_end"
# A whole disk carrying both a filesystem signature and a partition table
# is a hybrid ISO; it belongs to its partitions, not to us. A true
# superfloppy (mkfs straight onto /dev/sda, no table) has no
# ID_PART_TABLE_TYPE and still matches, which is the case this keeps working.
ENV{DEVTYPE}=="disk", ENV{ID_PART_TABLE_TYPE}!="", GOTO="tributary_storage_end"

RUN+="/usr/local/lib/tributary/drive-mount.sh %E{DEVNAME} %E{ID_FS_TYPE} %E{ID_FS_LABEL}"

LABEL="tributary_storage_end"
